## Summary

Alright, let's wrap this party up. In this post, we talked about why you should consider using Node for your next IoT project: It's easy to update over a network, the internet already speaks JavaScript, there are tons of existing libraries/plugins/APIs (including [CylonJS and Johnny-Five), and JavaScript is great at handling event-driven apps. We looked at a real-life Node-based IoT project, my IoT Kitty Litter Box. Then, we dug into the code base for the IoT Kitty Litter Box. We also discussed what to look for when selecting a database for IoT projects: It should be able to concurrently write data quickly, have a flexible schema, and be able to handle time series data.

